    <h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li><strong>Marking up content that isn't visible on the page</strong>. Google's guidelines require structured data to reflect what a visitor can actually see and read</li>
      <li><strong>Using FAQPage markup on marketing copy dressed up as questions</strong>, it should be genuine, distinct questions with real answers, not disguised ad copy</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting to update dates and prices</strong>, stale structured data (like an old price) can get a listing flagged or ignored</li>
      <li><strong>Stacking multiple conflicting schema types on one page</strong>, only mark up what the page is actually about</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Add This to Your Site</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li>Choose a schema type above and fill in the real details for your page</li>
      <li>Copy the generated code or download it as an HTML snippet</li>
      <li>Paste it into your page's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> (or anywhere in <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>)</li>
      <li>Test it with <a href="https://search.google.com/test/rich-results" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Google's Rich Results Test</a> before publishing</li>
      <li>Update the markup whenever the underlying page content changes</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related Questions About Schema Markup Generators</h2>
    <h3>Schema Markup Generator: The Four Types It Builds</h3>
    <p>This <strong>schema markup generator</strong> builds JSON-LD for four types: FAQPage, HowTo, LocalBusiness and Article. You fill in the details, then copy the code or download it as an HTML snippet. Our <a href="/blog/types-of-schema-markup">types of schema markup</a> guide explains the wider list.</p>
    <h3>Schema Code Generator: Where the Code Goes</h3>
    <p>Paste the generated code into the head of your page, or anywhere in the body, since search engines read JSON-LD from anywhere in the document. Test it, and update the markup whenever the page content changes.</p>
    <h3>Free Schema Markup Generator: Nothing Leaves Your Browser</h3>
    <p>The code is generated in your browser with JavaScript. Nothing is uploaded, logged or stored on any server, and you do not need an account or any coding knowledge. You get schema markup free of charge, so use this as your free schema markup generator when you want a fast, private result. You can use schema markup online this way without an account.</p>
    <h3>FAQ Schema Generator: What Google Shows Today</h3>
    <p>Google says the FAQ rich result is no longer shown in Search, starting 7 May 2026, and it has removed the documentation. A <strong>FAQ schema markup generator</strong> still produces valid markup, but you should not expect the dropdown in Google. If you add it, only mark up genuine questions and answers that are visible on the page. Check <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/updates" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Google's documentation updates</a> for the current position.</p>
    <h3>What This Schema Generator Covers</h3>
    <p>This generator covers FAQPage, HowTo, LocalBusiness and Article. It does not build WebSite or review markup. For a blog post, Article markup helps Google show better title text, images and dates, and for a business with a physical location, LocalBusiness markup can feed a knowledge panel with details such as hours. If you are looking for a website schema generator, note that it does not build WebSite markup.</p>
    <h3>How to Schema Generator: Check Your Markup Is Valid</h3>
    <p>Paste your page URL or the code into Google's Rich Results Test. It flags syntax errors and shows which rich results your page qualifies for. For a deeper structural check of existing markup, use our <a href="/tools/schema-markup-validator">schema markup validator</a>.</p>
    <h2>Free Schema Markup Generator FAQs</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <details>
        <summary>Do I need to know how to code to use this?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Fill in the plain-language form fields and the tool writes the correct JSON-LD syntax for you. Pasting the result onto your site is the only technical step, and it's the same as adding any other snippet of HTML.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Will adding schema markup guarantee rich results in Google?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Structured data makes your page eligible for rich results, it doesn't guarantee them. Google still decides whether and how to display them based on overall page quality and relevance.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool store or send my data anywhere?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Everything is generated in your browser with JavaScript, nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored on any server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I add more than one schema type to the same page?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Yes, technically, a page can carry multiple <code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;</code> blocks. Just make sure each one accurately describes real, visible content on that specific page.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where exactly should I paste the generated code?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Anywhere in your page's HTML, the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> is the most common convention, but search engines read JSON-LD from anywhere in the document.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do I check my schema markup is valid?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Paste your page URL or the code directly into <a href="https://search.google.com/test/rich-results" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Google's Rich Results Test</a>, it flags syntax errors and shows which rich results your page qualifies for.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does Google still show FAQ rich results?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Google says the FAQ rich result is no longer shown in Search results, starting 7 May 2026. FAQ markup from this tool is still valid, but it will not produce the dropdown in Google.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does the generator support review or website schema?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. It builds FAQPage, HowTo, LocalBusiness and Article markup.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

